 succeeded in 110ms:
<section class="competition">
<h2>The incumbent gets a turn</h2>
<p>SGS Search already markets exposure studies from target selection through pumps, laboratory analysis, interpretation and reporting. <!-- CHECK: 04-sgs-offer --> KWA describes the same four-step path, using a model where suitable and worker-worn pumps where it is not. <!-- CHECK: 04-kwa-offer --> ArboNed can add an occupational hygienist to an existing prevention relationship and publishes small blocks of expert time. <!-- CHECK: 04-arboned-price --> A local entrant does not possess a missing technical capability.</p>
<p>The internal substitute may be enough. A prevention employee can maintain the product list and certificates, while a hygienist validates a model and appears only for exceptions. Suppliers already owe training material, and the five-year certificate makes training administration intermittent rather than monthly. <!-- CHECK: 04-reach-detail --> A capable employer can therefore assemble the file from existing people and buy a specialist only when the model fails.</p>
<p>The partner has the strongest capture route. The hygienist owns credibility, method and Dutch management conversation. After the reader finds a Polish-speaking crew and organises the evidence, the partner can contract directly for the next review. A supplier can also refer only customers using its own system, narrowing independence. The defence is a multi-partner channel, controlled field method and customer contract. None is a deep moat.</p>
<p>The lead and chromium file is a later line, not support for this model. It adds medical coordination and different site types. Local-exhaust and respirator testing are also adjacent, but the reviewed Dutch record did not establish an interval that creates a recurring sale outside special regimes. <!-- CHECK: 04-adjacent-boundary --> Expansion should follow a paid request, not rescue weak diisocyanate economics.</p>
</section>

<div class="counter">
<div class="case-head"><span class="who">The capable failure</span></div>
<p>The company executes well. Contractors like the bilingual site work, the hygienist approves sound assessments, and two measurements uncover correctable exposure. Then the portfolio separates. Most stable booth processes close with a model and an internal action list. The complex open-spray jobs require several representative days and senior interpretation, so the hygienist takes more of the fee.</p>
<p>Meanwhile customers copy the training register, put review triggers into their RI&amp;E and call only after a material change. The five-year training cycle creates no annual retainer. The partner learns which crews value the work and offers them a wider occupational-health contract. Large providers spread travel and laboratory capacity across many hazards; the entrant carries idle equipment and fragmented site dates.</p>
<p>Nothing failed through careless selling or bad measurement. Good delivery taught stable customers to maintain the file, while difficult customers remained expert-heavy. The work survives as occasional bilingual field support to a hygienist. It may pay an attractive day rate, but fifty baselines and sixty reviews never appear. The model accepted by the Inspectorate did not eliminate work; it eliminated the repeatable middle.</p>
</div>

<section class="monday">
<h2>The first ninety days</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Days 1–15:</strong> recruit two independent Dutch-speaking occupational hygienists. Ask each to mark the scope boundary, method decision, signature, insurance and customer ownership. Stop if neither will supervise controlled fieldwork by the reader.</li>
<li><strong>Days 1–25:</strong> build thirty accounts across the ten prospect types and conduct ten English, Polish or partner-led Dutch interviews. Ask for the last assessment, model, measurement, certificate audit, provider, invoice and trigger. Ask two training providers and two associations for aggregate firm and certificate counts; record refusals and incompatible definitions.</li>
<li><strong>Days 15–35:</strong> have one hygienist review the desk triage, evidence request and one dummy report. Obtain insurance advice and a rental quotation. Cap partner, legal and travel spending at a <strong>€2,000 pre-evidence hurdle</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Days 25–55:</strong> offer the €1,250 desk triage to five qualified employers, 50% in advance. Also obtain comparable written quotes from two large providers, including what an existing arbodienst contract covers.</li>
<li><strong>Days 40–75:</strong> convert one triage into the €4,750 baseline. Observe a normal job beside the day job, with the hygienist's approved method. Rent instruments only if the assessment calls for measurement. Time founder, technician and hygienist work separately.</li>
<li><strong>Days 70–90:</strong> offer the same baseline to a second employer through a different route and quote the first real change review. Total spending before first customer payment is capped at <strong>€4,500</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Pass:</strong> two employers buy desk triage, one converts to a baseline, and a second accepts the baseline price through another route. The hygienist cost stays below one third of baseline revenue, and the first delivery identifies routine work a technician can repeat. <strong>Kill:</strong> no paid triage after five qualified offers; all five employers show a current accepted file inside an existing contract; comparable providers quote the full baseline below €3,000; or every credible hygienist insists on owning the customer. Also kill the direct route if Dutch management delivery cannot stay with the partner.</p>
</section>

<div class="verdict-box">
<h2>Verdict</h2>
<p class="verdict"><strong>TEST THROUGH A PARTNER</strong></p>
<p>The hygienist is both the competence gate and the Dutch commercial layer. The single assumption most able to change this verdict is that two independent contractors will pay the mature €5,500 baseline hurdle while letting the entrant retain the customer and enough routine fieldwork to reach a 49% gross-margin design.</p>
</div>

