     1	# Pitch 10 — The spray crew's file (Family A)
     2	
     3	**The unit and the file.** For one crew that sprays polyurethane foam, applies PU coatings or
     4	paints vehicles: the diisocyanate exposure assessment, the exposure register, the training
     5	records the REACH restriction requires, and the annual review; sold per crew per year (estimate
     6	€1,500–3,000, with measurement days extra). Second line, same kit and same customers: the lead
     7	and chromium file for blasting, demolition and strip-out crews.
     8	
     9	**The rule and the date.** Directive (EU) 2024/869, transposed in the Netherlands and in force
    10	9 April 2026, transitional values to 31 December 2028 (verified twice, Arboportaal and the
    11	legislative calendar); the REACH training duty for diisocyanate users since 24 August 2023, with
    12	a five-year certificate (verified).
    13	
    14	**Who buys.** Insulation contractors, coating applicators and body shops with five to fifty
    15	staff; many of the crews are Polish- and Ukrainian-speaking, and some of the firms are
    16	Polish-owned. Too small for the large occupational-health providers; exposed to the Labour
    17	Inspectorate after 2026.
    18	
    19	**Why you.** The crews are the reader's languages, and the training and the register are half
    20	conversation. Regulatory literacy reads the directive and the Dutch transposition directly;
    21	engineering habit runs the sampling protocol and the register. The Dutch gate is real and named:
    22	the Inspectorate expects the assessment at occupational-hygienist level, so a registered hygienist
    23	signs from the first sale. The reader sells and runs the file; the hygienist signs it.
    24	
    25	**The precedent.** Synergy Environmental Solutions Ltd, Stoke-on-Trent, incorporated 2012
    26	(Companies House, re-traced through two directories); EHS Analytical Solutions, San Diego, air
    27	sampling and blood-lead rounds (traced through federal contracting records); Respiratory
    28	Protective Assessment Ltd, Bristol, for the mask line.
    29	
    30	**The ninety-day test.** One paid exposure survey and training-record audit for one spray-foam
    31	contractor, with a hygienist on a day rate. Under €2,000 including the hygienist.
    32	
    33	**Steps two and three.** Step two: a retained hygienist and a book of twenty crews across foam,
    34	coating and body shops. Step three: the iron: local-exhaust-ventilation testing per system and
    35	respirator fit-testing per face, both on watch until a Dutch rule names them, and both sold to
    36	the same crews.
    37	
    38	**The AI line.** A sampling pump on a person on a site; AI writes the register and reads the
    39	transitional values. More rules, more fields, same site visit.
    40	
    41	**The kill fact.** The Inspectorate accepts modelled exposure without measurement, which the
    42	review found half true: models are accepted, but multi-day measurement is expected near the
    43	limits. If the large occupational-health providers bundle the file into their existing contracts
    44	at no charge, the niche closes. Two contractor calls and one provider's price list.
    45	
    46	**The number to settle first.** How many Dutch firms use diisocyanates in spray form, and how
    47	many have the REACH training certificates on file. The training providers know the second.
    48	
    49	**Verdict under the four assumptions.** Ranked tenth and last of the pitches: beside a job,
    50	capital under €5k plus a rented pump, no retraining, hands-on. It sits last because the Dutch gate
    51	is a partner rather than a paper. It rises if the reader takes the hygienist qualification, which
    52	is exactly the two-year retraining the assumptions rule out.
     1	# Edge inventory — draft for the author to correct (2026-09-03)
     2	
     3	What the reader has that a typical Dutch small-business starter does not. Every research brief
     4	under `drafts/research/` screens candidates against this list. Correct it; wrong lines here mislead
     5	every run downstream.
     6	
     7	| edge | what it is | what it is worth | what it is not |
     8	|---|---|---|---|
     9	| Languages | English (working), Russian and Polish (native-level or near), Dutch A2 | Customers, suppliers and staff in the Polish, Russian-speaking, Ukrainian and Belarusian communities in NL; Polish manufacturers as a supply side; cross-border B2B in English | Not a Dutch-facing consumer business; not regulated Dutch-language exams |
    10	| Background | Eastern European schooling and network; Belarusian origin; long residence in NL | Trust and reach inside diaspora and CEE trade that a Dutch founder cannot buy | Not a substitute for Dutch licences or local relationships |
    11	| Capital | ~EUR 100k, ceiling for the first test (to be confirmed) | Buys a small business with bank debt, a distribution stock position, one licensed setup | Not enough for housing property or a franchise fee plus fit-out |
    12	| Regulatory literacy | Reads EU and Dutch law and implementation directly; four books of it (CRA, Data Act, DPP, GTO, EUDI, machinery) | Compliance as a moat in distribution or services; sees deadlines before the market | Not legal advice; counsel still needed |
    13	| Engineering habit | Automates, integrates, measures; systems thinking | Operations that others run on spreadsheets and WhatsApp; evidence trails | Must not become an IT business again |
    14	| Research pipeline | The book machine: evidence-led screening, panel review, verdicts | Can screen 10 options for the cost of one consultant's afternoon | Screening is not selling |
    15	| Audience | ~30,000 LinkedIn followers, professional | A channel for B2B offers, recruiting partners, testing demand | Not a consumer channel; not to be turned into a content business |
    16	| Constraints | A2 Dutch; day job (?); family (?); risk appetite (?); retraining tolerance (?) | | The four open constraints: passive vs hands-on; alongside the job or replacing it; capital ceiling; two years of retraining or not |

